<section class="sd-hero"><div class="sd-w sd-hero-g"><div class="sd-page"><p class="sd-tag">Study skills that actually work</p><h1>Remember more.<br><mark>Study less.</mark></h1><p class="sd-lead">Studora is an independent study-skills site. We explain the techniques that research shows work best, such as spaced repetition, self-testing and good notes, and give you simple tools to use them.</p><p class="sd-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Plan my study</a><a class="sd-ln" href="memory-techniques-guide.html">How memory works &rarr;</a></p></div><figure class="sd-hero-f"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794338607990-c06a87729093?auto=format&fit=crop&w=560&h=640&q=75" alt="aerial view of people studying at wooden desks with green lamps" width="560" height="640" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption><b>Tip:</b> close the book and explain it out loud. That&rsquo;s retrieval practice.</figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section class="sd-s"><div class="sd-w"><div class="sd-ttl"><h2>Three methods with strong evidence</h2><p>If you only change three habits, change these.</p></div><div class="sd-meth">
<article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793296632405-c342a5535f94?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=280&q=75" alt="writing with a blue pen in a spiral notebook" width="420" height="280" loading="lazy"><div><h3><span>A</span>Test yourself</h3><p>Answer questions, use flashcards, or write what you remember before checking. Recall builds memory more than reviewing.</p></div></article>
<article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793363546942-3ae062e08ae5?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=280&q=75" alt="wall calendar with handwritten notes" width="420" height="280" loading="lazy"><div><h3><span>B</span>Space it out</h3><p>Short reviews spread over days and weeks beat one long session the night before.</p></div></article>
<article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794515708849-86d1a7b69ec5?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=280&q=75" alt="stack of four books with colourful spines" width="420" height="280" loading="lazy"><div><h3><span>C</span>Mix it up</h3><p>Alternate between related topics and problem types to learn when to use each method.</p></div></article></div></div></section>

<section class="sd-tl" id="tools"><div class="sd-w"><div class="sd-ttl sd-ttl-w"><h2>Study tools</h2><p>Plan reviews, sessions and reading in a minute.</p></div><div class="sd-tools">
<div class="sd-tool"><div class="sd-tab">Tool 1</div><h3>Spaced Repetition Scheduler</h3><label for="sr-d">Date you first learned it<input id="sr-d" type="date"></label><label for="sr-x">Exam date (optional)<input id="sr-x" type="date"></label><div class="sd-out" id="sr-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="sd-tool"><div class="sd-tab">Tool 2</div><h3>Study Session Planner</h3><label for="ss-h">Time available (hours)<input id="ss-h" type="number" min="0.5" max="12" step="0.5" value="3"></label><label for="ss-f">Focus block<select id="ss-f"><option value="25">25 min work / 5 min break</option><option value="50" selected>50 min work / 10 min break</option><option value="90">90 min work / 20 min break</option></select></label><div class="sd-out" id="ss-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="sd-tool"><div class="sd-tab">Tool 3</div><h3>Reading Time Estimator</h3><label for="rt-p">Pages to read<input id="rt-p" type="number" min="1" max="2000" value="40"></label><label for="rt-t">Type of text<select id="rt-t"><option value="300">Light reading (about 300 words/page)</option><option value="450" selected>Textbook (about 450 words/page)</option><option value="550">Dense academic (about 550 words/page)</option></select></label><label for="rt-s">Reading pace<select id="rt-s"><option value="150">Careful, taking notes</option><option value="220" selected>Steady</option><option value="300">Quick skim</option></select></label><div class="sd-out" id="rt-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="sd-s"><div class="sd-w sd-week"><div><div class="sd-ttl sd-ttl-l"><h2>A sample study week</h2><p>One subject, about an hour a day, built around retrieval and spacing.</p></div><p>Notice that new material and review sit side by side every day. Reviews get shorter as the memory gets stronger.</p><a class="btn sd-btn-o" href="note-taking-guide.html">Take better notes first</a></div>
<div class="sd-grid" role="table" aria-label="Sample study week"><div class="sd-gr sd-gh" role="row"><span role="columnheader">Day</span><span role="columnheader">New material</span><span role="columnheader">Review</span></div><div class="sd-gr" role="row"><span role="cell">Mon</span><span role="cell">Topic 1: read + notes</span><span role="cell">&mdash;</span></div><div class="sd-gr" role="row"><span role="cell">Tue</span><span role="cell">Topic 2</span><span role="cell">Quiz on Topic 1</span></div><div class="sd-gr" role="row"><span role="cell">Wed</span><span role="cell">Topic 3</span><span role="cell">Flashcards: 1 &amp; 2</span></div><div class="sd-gr" role="row"><span role="cell">Thu</span><span role="cell">Practice problems</span><span role="cell">Explain Topic 1 aloud</span></div><div class="sd-gr" role="row"><span role="cell">Fri</span><span role="cell">Topic 4</span><span role="cell">Mixed quiz 1&ndash;3</span></div><div class="sd-gr" role="row"><span role="cell">Sat</span><span role="cell">Rest or light reading</span><span role="cell">Short recap</span></div><div class="sd-gr" role="row"><span role="cell">Sun</span><span role="cell">&mdash;</span><span role="cell">Past paper, timed</span></div></div></div></section>

<section class="sd-s sd-sky"><div class="sd-w"><div class="sd-ttl"><h2>Read the guides</h2></div><div class="sd-gds"><a href="memory-techniques-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794584171673-a957ccf82183?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=380&q=75" alt="circular tiered lecture hall in black and white" width="640" height="380" loading="lazy"><div><small>Guide 1 &middot; 9 min</small><h3>The Memory Techniques Guide</h3><p>Retrieval practice, spacing, interleaving and memory aids that help facts stick.</p></div></a><a href="note-taking-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793987057647-1db793fdd4c3?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=380&q=75" alt="handwritten scientific notes pinned to a board" width="640" height="380" loading="lazy"><div><small>Guide 2 &middot; 8 min</small><h3>The Note-Taking Guide</h3><p>Cornell notes, outlines, mind maps and how to turn notes into revision.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="sd-s"><div class="sd-w sd-fq"><div class="sd-ttl sd-ttl-l"><h2>Student questions</h2><p>Straight answers to common study questions.</p></div><div class="sd-faq"><details open><summary>What is spaced repetition?</summary><p>Reviewing material at increasing intervals, for example after one day, three days, a week and two weeks. Each review comes just as you are about to forget, which makes the memory stronger and longer-lasting.</p></details><details><summary>Is re-reading my notes a good way to study?</summary><p>It feels productive but is one of the weaker methods. Testing yourself, by answering questions or explaining a topic from memory, is far more effective for long-term learning.</p></details><details><summary>How long should a study session be?</summary><p>Many people focus well for 25&ndash;50 minutes followed by a 5&ndash;10 minute break. After three or four sessions, take a longer break of 20&ndash;30 minutes.</p></details><details><summary>What is the Cornell note-taking method?</summary><p>A page layout with a narrow cue column on the left, a wide notes column on the right and a summary box at the bottom. You take notes on the right, write questions on the left and summarise at the end.</p></details><details><summary>Should I study one subject at a time?</summary><p>Mixing related topics or problem types in one session, called interleaving, often leads to better long-term results than practising one type over and over, even though it feels harder.</p></details><details><summary>Does sleep really affect learning?</summary><p>Yes. Sleep helps consolidate what you studied during the day. Cutting sleep to study longer often means you remember less of what you studied.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="sd-nl"><div class="sd-w sd-nl-in"><div><h2>The Studora Note</h2><p>One study technique, explained in five minutes, every month.</p></div><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@studora.com?subject=The%20Studora%20Note&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></section>

